{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793383605,"nonce":"of0Gp+vDX4/6TszZsV5eA8KeLBQ=","message":"nan8 (../nan8) read. It is the planned provider layer above dynASB, but today it is a Phase 0 skeleton: about 700 lines across nine crates, a trait and policy types, no backend implemented, not a git repository. nan8_executor_core defines Executor { deploy(DeploySpec) -\u003e ActorHandle, undeploy, send(port, Message), subscribe(port) -\u003e receiver } with Code = DynAsbSource{runtime, source} | OciImage{image, entrypoint}, and per-backend Policy types (DynAsbPolicy: runtime, warm_pool, isolation, restart; MicrosandboxPolicy: image plus pooling strategy Pool, SnapshotFork, TenantAffinity, LanguageRouted). Backends: dynASB default, microsandbox (the same project MSB is) secondary. Open question Q6 there proposes a capabilities() method (supports_gpu_passthrough, supports_snapshot_fork, supports_warm_pool). Fit for RayDE workspaces: the trait is an actor executor (deploy code, message ports), not a session host. It has no volumes, no long-lived process with stdio and port relay, no snapshot or restore call, no resource sizing and no GPU request, all of which rayde-runner needs (create from a toolchain template, persistent fenced workspace and document volumes, relay to rayde-session, restore after VM loss, GPU option). Observation: RayDE's runner and nan8 both sit on the same two engines (microsandbox and dynASB), so a lower sandbox-provider interface, below nan8's Executor, could serve both: create(template, resources, volumes, gpu?) -\u003e sandbox; exec or spawn with stdio; port relay; snapshot, restore, fork; stop and reclaim; capabilities(). nan8's Executor would then be one client of it and rayde-runner another, instead of RayDE growing a third abstraction. Decision still open: draft that interface (a short doc and a Rust trait), with MSB as the first implementation since RayDE already runs on it, then dynASB.","files":null}]}